From b16f4ba490c1c5f6d85201e1b9465c72c2cecd7d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 00:04:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] test(gateway): retain socket custody in crash collision probe
 [daveey]

The old controlled-reproduction test forced the restarted dashboard to bind
the previous aiohttp API port. That port was no longer reserved, so another
owner made the injection itself fail EADDRINUSE before daemon readiness.
PR3125's failing head and main have identical probe/runtime bytes; its
annotation changes did not introduce this fixture's fixed-port claim.

Replace that injection with continuous socket custody: bind once in the
parent, let the selected predecessor serve the real aiohttp API through an
inherited descriptor, then retain the parent's duplicate across SIGKILL.
Both successors retain ordinary port0 allocation and fresh listener receipts.
Cover collisions at both restart boundaries; assert the predecessor actually
used the retained socket and successors avoided it. Preserve all existing
unknown-execution, safe-queue, retained-result and exactly-once assertions.
Descriptor sharing is POSIX-only; ordinary portable recovery is unchanged.
No production or retry/deadline changes.

Proof: restoring the old fixed-dashboard injection under controlled socket
ownership fails both cases at listener.bind with errno98. Removing fresh
log offsets fails both collision cases. Canonical owning suite (3 cases)
passes GREEN and three further repetitions with HERMES_TEST_FILE_RETRIES=0.
Full scripts/check --base origin/main passes, including staged new fixture.
